0}, "rating_margin": -0.36, "sigma_race": 5.6, "sigma_total": 6.01, "baseline_margin": -0.49, "analyst_adjustment": -0.01, "key_factors": ["Lombardo's incumbency in a slightly Republican-leaning state offsets a Democratic-tilting midterm environment", "Recent polls are razor-thin: Noble shows Lombardo +1/+2 in late September, Emerson had Ford +2", "Betting markets lean narrowly to Ford (50-54%), against the slight polling edge", "Independent Danielle Ford and Nevada's large nonpartisan bloc add late-deciding uncertainty"], "rationale": "Lombardo's incumbency and Nevada's slight Republican lean roughly cancel a favorable midterm environment for Democrats; the newest polls give him a one-to-two-point edge while markets narrowly favor Ford, leaving a coin flip.", "watch": "Late movement among nonpartisan voters, or the parole-attack line against Ford gaining traction, could break the dead heat in either direction.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Incumbent Joe Lombardo (R) is running."]}, "overview": "Nevada's governor's race is a genuine coin flip. Incumbent Republican Joe Lombardo seeks a second term against Democratic Attorney General Aaron Ford, with independent Danielle Ford also on the ballot. Seven race raters call it a tossup, and one adds a slight Republican tilt. The most recent polls are tight: Noble Predictive Insights put Lombardo up one among registered voters and two among likely voters in late September, while Emerson College had Aaron Ford up two in early September. Averages across outlets land between even and Republican plus one.\n\nThe fundamentals cut both ways. Lombardo carries the usual incumbent advantage in a state that leaned Republican by a point at the last presidential election, but midterm conditions historically punish the party holding the White House. Betting markets nudge toward Ford, pricing him between 50 and 54 percent. With no debates scheduled and a large nonpartisan electorate, late movement could decide this.", "history": [{